<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1977</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Madres de Plaza de Mayo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo are an Argentine association of mothers of people who disappeared under the military dictatorship. From April 1977 they marched every Thursday in the Plaza de Mayo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, wearing white headscarves, to demand news of their children, although founder Azucena Villaflor was herself abducted and killed. They became a worldwide symbol of human rights activism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mothers-of-the-plaza-de-mayo.jpg" alt="Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archivo Hasenberg-Quaretti · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2%C2%AA_Marcha_de_la_Resistencia_9_y_10_diciembre_1982.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
